  {
    q: 'Which port should I use when shipping from China to Portugal?',
    a: 'Sines (PTSIE) is the deep-water default for mainline containers and the first deep-sea European call on the Cape routing, Lisbon (PTLIS) serves the capital market, and Leixões (PTLEI) serves Porto and the industrial north ~300 km above Lisbon — so the port follows the destination rather than a single national gateway.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How does Portugal\u2019s landed cost compare with Spain\u2019s?',
    a: 'On a $10,000 CIF at 5% duty, Portugal\u2019s 23% VAT lands at $12,915 versus Spain\u2019s 21% at $12,705 — a $210 gap, exactly 2 points on the duty-inclusive base — before any inland or destination fees. Against Germany\u2019s 19%, the same shipment is $420 higher.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I ship from China to Portugal by rail?',
    a: 'Yes, but not seamlessly — Spain and Portugal run 1668mm Iberian gauge against the standard 1435mm the New Silk Road uses, so a China-Europe train must transload at the Spanish border before any rail leg into Portugal. In practice most rail cargo trucks from Madrid, so the realistic rail total is roughly 18–22 days to Spain plus ~1–2 days transload/truck, at roughly 2–3× the sea rate.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why is Sines a strategic gateway for China cargo?',
    a: 'Because it is on the Atlantic: on the Cape-of-Good-Hope routing a China vessel reaches Sines before Rotterdam or Hamburg, and its Terminal XXI (≈17.5 m draft, approximate) takes the ultra-large vessels the smaller Iberian ports cannot. Direct China calls are thinner than the northern range, so much cargo transships at Algeciras or Tanger Med first — but for Lisbon and the south, Sines usually beats a northern-range discharge.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need an EORI to import into Portugal?',
    a: 'Yes — one EORI (Economic Operators Registration and Identification) number is required for any import into the EU, and it must exist before the goods arrive; zero Portuguese imports clear without it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the difference between FCL and LCL for China to Portugal?',
    a: 'FCL is priced per container (Iberian corridor benchmark 20ft $1,300–$5,000, 40ft $1,800–$7,000; Sines-specific figures are not published and must be requested) and LCL per cubic metre ($70–$170/CBM), with the planning break-even near 15 CBM — below that LCL is usually cheaper, above it FCL starts to win.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What documents are needed for Portuguese customs clearance?',
    a: 'The core set is four documents plus one classification — commercial invoice, bill of lading (or rail/CMR consignment note for rail cargo), packing list and the correct 10-digit TARIC code, backed by your EORI number. Regulated products add CE marking evidence and chemicals add REACH registration.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I defer VAT when importing into Portugal?',
    a: 'Portugal, like other EU states, allows EU customs warehousing (procedures 42/63) to defer import VAT until goods are released for free circulation — a working-capital lever for importers who hold inventory or re-export within the EU, worth more cash at 23% VAT than in a 19% or 20% market.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What are CE marking and REACH, and do they apply in Portugal?',
    a: 'CE marking is the EU conformity mark required for regulated products — electronics, toys, machinery and medical devices — and REACH is the EU chemical regulation requiring registration for chemical substances. Both apply in Portugal exactly as in every member state.',
  },
];

