<BaseLayout
  title="Shipping from China to Portugal: Sines PTSIE, 23% VAT"
  description="China to Portugal freight guide: 23% VAT on EU common duty, Sines PTSIE gateway, ~30–38-day Cape sea transit and the 1668mm Iberian-gauge rail wall."
  canonical={pageUrl}
  ogType="article"
  schema={schema}
>
  <div class="page-header">
    <div class="container">
      <Breadcrumb
        items={[
          { label: 'Europe', href: '/europe/' },
          { label: 'Shipping from China to Portugal' },
        ]}
      />
      <span class="badge badge--amber">September 2026 updated</span>
      <h1>Shipping from China to Portugal: Sines PTSIE gateway, 23% VAT, and the Iberian-gauge rail question</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        A one-page freight brief for importers moving cargo from Shanghai, Ningbo-Zhoushan, Shenzhen
        and the wider China origin set to Sines, Lisbon and Leixões — with the three-port geography,
        the roughly 30–38-day sea window via the Cape, the 18–22-day rail alternative that stops at the
        1668mm Iberian gauge, EU common duty plus 23% VAT, the EORI gate, and the hidden destination
        charges that shape the Portuguese landed cost.
      </p>
      <blockquote class="tldr">
        <p><strong>TL;DR:</strong> Portugal is entered through <strong>Sines (PTSIE)</strong>, a deep-water Atlantic gateway reached <strong>ahead of the northern range</strong> on the Cape routing — but the port is not the destination, and Lisbon (capital) and Leixões (Porto) split the market. The statutory math compounds: 5% duty + 23% VAT = 1.05 × 1.23 = <strong>×1.2915</strong>, so a $10,000 CIF becomes <strong>$12,915</strong> before destination fees — <strong>$210 higher</strong> than Spain's 21% VAT and <strong>$420 higher</strong> than Germany's 19% on the same base. Rail lands in <strong>18–22 days</strong> to Madrid but then hits the <strong>1668mm Iberian gauge</strong> — the same gauge wall the New Silk Road meets on its eastern edge, repeated on Europe's western edge — so most rail cargo trucks the last leg. Sea runs <strong>30–38 days</strong> via the Cape.</p>
      </blockquote>
      <p class="small muted">
        Confidence badges separate verified figures from indicative planning notes. Portugal's FCL/LCL
        benchmarks come from the WorldFreightHub Europe route data Valencia Iberian corridor (MEDIUM) and
        are labelled as reference only, while Sines-, Lisbon- and Leixões-specific, rail, air, express and
        DDP dollar figures were not published in the snapshot and are shown as request-for-quote markers.
        Treat every LOW-confidence figure as an indicative planning input and verify with your carrier
        before relying on it.
      </p>

      <div class="fact-grid mt-3">
