  <section class="section container">
    <h2>1. Why Portugal deserves its own China routing lens</h2>
    <p>
      Portugal is the Atlantic end of Iberia, and it breaks the two habits that importers bring from the
      northern range. The country data shows <strong>Lisbon</strong> as the capital, the currency as
      <strong>EUR</strong>, and a <strong>23% standard VAT</strong> layered on top of the
      <strong>EU Common Customs Tariff</strong> — a duty line that is uniform across every member state
      and varies only by HS code, typically <strong>0–12% for consumer goods</strong> with no China–EU
      free-trade agreement in force.
    </p>
    <p>
      The first habit to break is defaulting to Rotterdam or Valencia. Portugal's deep-water gateway
      <strong>Sines (PTSIE)</strong> sits on the Atlantic, further west than any northern-range port — so
      on the Cape-of-Good-Hope routing a China vessel reaches Sines <em>before</em> Rotterdam or Hamburg.
      That is a structural time advantage the Mediterranean ports do not share, and it is the single
      biggest reason to price Sines before assuming an Iberian transshipment or a northern-range discharge
      for Portugal-bound cargo.
    </p>
    <p>
      The second habit is treating Portugal as a rail-through market. Spain and Portugal run
      <strong>1668mm Iberian gauge</strong> on their conventional network, against the
      <strong>1435mm standard gauge</strong> the New Silk Road uses. A China-Europe train therefore cannot
      roll into Portugal without a transload at the Spanish border — the same gauge wall the corridor meets
      on its eastern edge at Małaszewicze, repeated on Europe's western edge. That single fact changes the
      rail-versus-sea decision for Portugal in a way it does not for Germany or Poland.
    </p>
    <p>
      On the commercial side the honest position mirrors Sweden: <strong>FCL and LCL ranges are published
      only as an Iberian corridor Valencia reference</strong> (MEDIUM confidence), while Sines-, Lisbon-
      and Leixões-specific, rail, air, express and DDP dollar figures are not — so those lines stay
      explicit request-for-quote markers instead of borrowed numbers.
    </p>
  </section>

