  <section class="section container">
    <h2>3. Transit times by origin port and mode</h2>
    <p>
      The China-to-Portugal clock has two beats for ocean cargo: the <strong>sea leg to the Portuguese
      Atlantic gateways</strong>, then the <strong>inland leg to the final site</strong>. The WorldFreightHub
      Europe route data puts sea transit at roughly <strong>30–38 days via the Cape of Good Hope</strong>
      from Shanghai or Ningbo-Zhoushan to Sines or Lisbon. Because Sines sits on the Atlantic, it is reached
      ahead of the northern range on the Cape routing, but direct China calls to Portuguese ports are
      thinner than at Rotterdam or Hamburg, so transshipment via Algeciras or Tanger Med is common and adds
      a move. Rail from Xi'an or Chengdu runs <strong>18–22 days</strong> to Madrid, then the
      <strong>1668mm Iberian gauge</strong> forces a transload and typically a ~1–2 day truck into Portugal.
      Portuguese air transit is not published, so that line stays explicit instead of borrowing a
      neighbouring airport figure.
    </p>

