    <div class="note mt-2">
      <strong>Sea planning default:</strong> Shanghai, Ningbo-Zhoushan and Shenzhen to Sines/Lisbon are
      recorded at roughly 30–38 days on the current Cape routing, plus the inland leg. Rail is roughly
      18–22 days to Madrid plus ~1–2 days transload/truck across the Iberian gauge. Both are planning
      estimates, not carrier promises — for air, express or a specific door-to-door move, request a
      routing quote before committing to a date. Note that China–Europe rail runs 1435 mm standard gauge
      into Spain, where the conventional Iberian network is 1668 mm — the same regauge/transshipment
      problem the corridor meets on its eastern broad-gauge edge, mirrored on the west.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>4. Sea vs rail vs air: Portugal's true door-to-door decision</h2>
      <p>
        Portugal's rail story changes the standard sea-versus-rail calculus. In Germany or Poland the
        question is clean — rail saves time at 2–3× the sea rate. In Portugal the rail leg stops at the
        <strong>1668mm Iberian gauge</strong>, so the honest comparison is
        <strong>sea to Sines (~30–38 days + inland)</strong> against
        <strong>rail to Madrid (18–22 days) + transload + ~1–2 days truck</strong> — roughly 20–24 days
        door-to-door by the rail route, at roughly 2–3× the sea rate plus the transload handling cost.
      </p>

      <DataTable
        columns={[
          { key: 'mode', label: 'Mode' },
          { key: 'bestFor', label: 'Best for' },
          { key: 'indicativeTime', label: 'Indicative time' },
