      />
      <SourceList sources={modeSources} title="Sources — sea vs rail vs air decision" />

      <div class="note mt-2">
        <strong>Critical point — the rail premium does not pay for itself on financing alone:</strong>
        the rail route saves roughly 10–18 days (20–24 versus ~30–38 sea plus inland) at roughly 2–3× the
        sea rate, plus a gauge transload. At an 8% cost of capital a $50,000 container costs ≈ $11/day in
        transit, so ~14 days is only about <strong>$154 of financing saved</strong> — which alone does not
        pay the rail premium. Rail is justified instead by <strong>stockout avoidance and market
        timing</strong> on high-value or seasonal cargo. Air is the extreme-time option, with no verified
        Portuguese rate or transit published, so request that as an itemised quote rather than assuming a
        neighbouring airport figure.
      </div>

      <div class="grid grid--3 mt-2">
        <Card title="Choose sea when...">
          <ul>
            <li>Cost per kg matters more than the ~30–38-day sea window plus the Portuguese inland leg.</li>
            <li>The cargo is heavy, palletised or container-sized and can plan ahead.</li>
            <li>You can accept a Sines/Lisbon discharge plus the inland rail/truck to your site.</li>
          </ul>
        </Card>
        <Card title="Choose rail when...">
          <ul>
            <li>Stockouts or seasonal windows are the risk and ~20–24 days beats ~30–38 sea plus inland.</li>
            <li>You accept the 1668mm gauge transload at the Spanish border and its handling cost.</li>
            <li>You accept roughly 2–3× sea cost for ~10–18 days of time saved.</li>
          </ul>
        </Card>
        <Card title="Choose air when...">
          <ul>
            <li>The cargo is time-critical, light or high-value.</li>
            <li>The value of a missed sale exceeds the air premium.</li>
            <li>You have a per-kg quote that beats the cost of delay.</li>
          </ul>
        </Card>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>5. Ports: China origin ports, Sines, Lisbon and the Leixões split</h2>
    <p>
      The origin side is the familiar China port hierarchy: Shanghai and Ningbo-Zhoushan anchor the
      East China ocean services, while Shenzhen and Guangzhou cover South China and Qingdao, Tianjin
      and Xiamen provide northern/southeast alternatives. The destination side is Portugal's three-port
      geometry: <strong>Sines (PTSIE)</strong> on the Atlantic south of Lisbon is the deep-water mainline
      gateway, <strong>Lisbon (PTLIS)</strong> is the capital port, and <strong>Leixões (PTLEI)</strong>
      serves Porto and the industrial north roughly 300 km above Lisbon. None of the three is the final
      destination for most Portuguese cargo — the inland leg is what the ocean quote omits.
    </p>

