  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>6. Cost composition and the hidden charges that competitors miss</h2>
      <p>
        Portugal's statutory stack is only the visible top layer: <strong>EU common duty on CIF</strong>
        (typically 0–12% by HS code), then <strong>23% VAT on the duty-inclusive base</strong>. Below
        that line sit the charges that are unique to Portugal — the <strong>inland leg from Sines to
        Lisbon (~150 km) or Leixões (~300 km)</strong> and the <strong>Iberian-gauge transload</strong> on
        any rail cargo — plus the operational charges the verified snapshot does not quantify: THC,
        documentation, inspection, port storage, demurrage and detention. This is where a cheap freight
        quote becomes an expensive clearance.
      </p>

      <div class="note">
        <strong>Baseline statutory landed cost = (CIF + duty) × 1.23.</strong><br />
        Duty varies by HS code, so there is no single multiplier like the Gulf's 5%. For an illustrative
        USD 10,000 CIF shipment at an assumed 5% duty: USD 500 duty gives a USD 10,500 duty-inclusive
        base, then 23% VAT of USD 2,415, giving USD 12,915 before inland and destination fees. The
        compound is <strong>1.05 × 1.23 = ×1.2915</strong>, not the 28% you would get by simply adding
        the two headline rates. Rates and duty lines are subject to change — verify the current duty line
        and VAT treatment with the Autoridade Tributária e Aduaneira before relying on this example.
      </div>

      <h3 class="mt-3">Full cost stack</h3>
      <DataTable
        columns={[
          { key: 'item', label: 'Cost component' },
          { key: 'who', label: 'Who charges it' },
          { key: 'magnitude', label: 'Indicative magnitude' },
          { key: 'confidence', label: 'Confidence', type: 'confidence' },
        ]}
        rows={costRows}
        caption="The Valencia Iberian corridor reference, EU duty and 23% VAT are MEDIUM/HIGH confidence from route/customs sources. Unquantified Portuguese and port fees are LOW confidence because the snapshot did not publish amounts."
      />
      <SourceList sources={costSources} title="Sources — landed cost & customs" />

      <h3 class="mt-3">What most guides skip</h3>
      <p>
        Five lines change the Portuguese landed cost and rarely appear on a standard ocean quote:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Sines is the first deep-sea European call on the Cape routing — use it.</strong>
          On the Cape-of-Good-Hope routing a China vessel reaches the Iberian Atlantic before the English
          Channel, so a Sines discharge can beat a Rotterdam transshipment for Portugal-bound cargo — yet
          most importers still default to Rotterdam or Valencia out of habit.</li>
        <li><strong>The 1668mm Iberian gauge is a real cost, not a footnote.</strong> Any China-Europe
          rail leg into Portugal must transload from 1435mm standard gauge at the Spanish border — a
          handling step and fee that a Germany or Poland rail quote does not carry.</li>
        <li><strong>The inland leg from Sines is not in the ocean quote.</strong> A Sines freight figure
          ends at the quay; the ~150 km to Lisbon or ~300 km to the Porto region is a separate charge that
          must be added before the quote is usable.</li>
        <li><strong>23% VAT is two points above Spain's 21%.</strong> On a $10,000 CIF at 5% duty that is
          $210 more in landed tax than shipping into Spain — a reason cross-Iberia routing decisions are
          tax-sensitive, not just freight-sensitive.</li>
        <li><strong>VAT deferral is available.</strong> EU customs warehousing (procedures 42/63) defers
          import VAT until goods are released for free circulation — useful for inventory holders and
          re-exporters, and worth more cash at 23% than in a 19% or 20% market.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="mt-3">Demurrage vs detention: two clocks, two payees</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Demurrage</strong> is the charge the <strong>terminal</strong> applies when import cargo
        stays in the port beyond the allowed free time after discharge. <strong>Detention</strong> is the
        charge the <strong>ocean carrier</strong> (or rail operator) applies when the importer keeps the
        container beyond the equipment free time after collection. They sound similar, but they are
        separate contracts, separate free-time allowances and separate per-day rates — and in a
        transshipment-heavy route like Portugal the detention clock can also run against the feeder carrier
        as well as the mainline line.
      </p>
      <p>
        Portuguese and Sines/Lisbon/Leixões free-time periods and per-day amounts are not published in the
        verified snapshot, so do not assume a Rotterdam or Valencia schedule applies. Before booking, ask
        the carrier and the forwarder to put the Portuguese free time, the inland free time, demurrage rate
        and detention rate in writing. The most reliable avoidance tactic is documentary readiness:
        register the EORI early, lock the TARIC code, prepare the invoice/packing list/B-L and file the
        Portuguese declaration promptly so release happens before either free-time clock runs out.
      </p>

      <h3 class="mt-3">Hidden charges to ask for on the quote</h3>
      <p>
        Request an itemised schedule that separates each destination line. A quote that only says "freight
        + customs" can hide <strong>THC</strong> at Sines/Lisbon/Leixões, a <strong>documentation fee</strong>,
        a possible <strong>customs inspection fee</strong>, <strong>port storage</strong>, the
        <strong>inland Portuguese leg</strong>, the <strong>Iberian-gauge transload</strong> on any rail
        cargo, and the <strong>demurrage/detention</strong> exposure if release is delayed. Without those
        lines, a dollar-level freight quote cannot be compared or used for landed-cost budgeting.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>7. Compliance: Portugal duty, 23% VAT, EORI &amp; conformity</h2>

    <h3>Tax and duty</h3>
    <p>
      Portugal applies the <strong>EU Common Customs Tariff</strong>, so duty is identical across member
      states and depends on the HS code — typically <strong>0–12% for consumer goods</strong>, with no
      China–EU free-trade agreement in force. Import <strong>23% VAT</strong> (13% and 6% reduced on
      some goods) is then charged on the <strong>duty-inclusive value</strong>, so the baseline statutory
      calculation is <code>(CIF + duty) × 1.23</code>. Specific goods can still carry different duty lines
      or VAT treatment, so the HS code remains the first compliance decision.
    </p>

    <h3>EORI — register before arrival</h3>
    <p>
      The <strong>EORI number</strong> is mandatory for any import into the EU and must be registered
      <em>before</em> the goods arrive. It is the single most common first-import stumbling block for
      China-to-Portugal cargo, so start the registration before the vessel or train departs, not while it
      is discharging at Sines or Lisbon.
    </p>

    <h3>TARIC classification and documents</h3>
    <p>
      Portugal classifies goods under the <strong>10-digit TARIC code</strong>, which drives the duty
      line, VAT treatment, permits and restricted screening. The standard document set is a commercial
      invoice, bill of lading (or rail/CMR consignment note for rail cargo), packing list and the correct
      TARIC classification, backed by your EORI number. Confirm the current declaration workflow with a
      licensed Portuguese broker.
    </p>

    <h3>CE marking and REACH</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>CE marking</strong> is the EU conformity mark required for a wide range of regulated
      products — electronics, toys, machinery and medical devices, among others. <strong>REACH</strong> is
      the EU chemical regulation requiring registration for chemical substances. Both apply in Portugal
      exactly as in every member state, so confirm the applicability and the required technical file
      before shipment.
    </p>

    <h3>VAT deferral via EU customs warehousing</h3>
    <p>
      Portugal, like other EU states, allows <strong>EU customs warehousing (procedures 42/63)</strong> to
      defer import VAT until goods are released for free circulation — a working-capital lever for
      importers who hold inventory or re-export within the EU. Given Portugal's 23% VAT tier, the deferral
      is worth more cash than in a 19% or 20% market, so ask your broker about the procedure before
      assuming VAT must be paid at the quay.
    </p>

    <h3>SABER/SASO is Saudi-only</h3>
    <p>
      SABER and SASO are <strong>Saudi-only</strong> conformity systems and do not apply to Portugal or the
      EU. Do not copy a Saudi SABER/SASO step into a Portuguese quote. Portugal uses CE marking and REACH
      instead, and any product-specific approval should be confirmed with the Autoridade Tributária e
      Aduaneira or your broker.
    </p>

    <h3>De-minimis and IOSS</h3>
    <p>
      The EU abolished the €22 duty-free threshold in 2021, so import VAT now applies from the first euro
      on most consignments. The <strong>€150</strong> figure is the VAT-related threshold, and the
      <strong>IOSS</strong> scheme simplifies VAT on sub-€150 consignments sold to EU buyers. Confirm the
      current thresholds and whether IOSS applies to your channel with the Portuguese customs authority
      before relying on these figures.
    </p>

    <SourceList sources={complianceSources} title="Sources — Portugal customs, duty, VAT & conformity" />
  </section>

